{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "XP and UML?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 12}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about XP or UML."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Give detailed information related to UML and common related topics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations. Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob12re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Both as an Adjective "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>The word <strong>both</strong> has different grammatical functions in English, one of the most common is its use as an <b>adjective</b>.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>As an&nbsp;<i>adjective</i>,&nbsp;<strong>both</strong>&nbsp;replaces two objects before a noun and it has the meaning of <i>one and the other</i> or <i>two together</i>.<br /> <br />e.g.&nbsp;<br /> <i>The company had to design two different programs today,</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>both</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>programs are important to make the company work.</i></p> <p><strong><i>Both</i></strong><i> tools are key in software developing. &nbsp;</i></p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>It&rsquo;s important to note that <strong>both</strong> can also be either a pronoun or a conjunction. Let&rsquo;s check the differences:</p> <p>As a&nbsp;<i>pronoun</i>&nbsp;<strong>both</strong>&nbsp;replaces two nouns.<br /> <br />e.g.<br /> <i>The company hired two different companies to develop the software;</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>both</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>were really good options.</i></p> <p>As a&nbsp;<i>correlative conjunction</i>,&nbsp;<strong>both&hellip; and&hellip;</strong>, the elements that are after&nbsp;<strong>both</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>and</strong>&nbsp;must be parallel, that means they must have some relation in terms of balance.<br /> <br />e.g.<br /> <i>She is</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>both</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>a programmer</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>and</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>analyst.</i><i><br />The company determined&nbsp;<strong>both</strong>&nbsp;to analyze the market and to develop&nbsp;<strong>and</strong>&nbsp;app for the company</i>.<br /> </p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Same way, some people can agree with the fact that both approaches are essential when designing software products as they both can relate to AM (Agile Modeling) principles."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some others consider it is advisable not to combine them because they are both alike in some aspects."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Tenses Review"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Modal auxiliary verbs, simple and continuous tenses have some similarities and differences, let's compare them. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>Modal Verbs</b><br><br>Among the modal auxiliary verbs, the most common ones are those expressing ability and/or probability, for example <strong>can</strong> and <strong>could</strong>.<br/><br/> When they express probabilities, they can be used interchangeably.<br/> E.g. <br/> <span class=\"italic\">This program <strong>can</strong> run in any OS, just give it a try.</span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">This program <strong>could</strong> run in any OS, just give it a try.</span><br/><br/> When they are used to talk about abilities, <strong>could</strong> is the past form of <strong>can</strong>.<br/> E.g.<br/> <span class=\"italic\">This diagram <strong>can</strong> help you organize your ideas. (you can use it)</span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">That diagram <strong>could</strong> help you organize your ideas.(you didn't use it)</span><br/><br/> <strong>Be able</strong> is a similar expression that means <strong>can</strong> when it expresses ability.<br/> E.g.<br/> <span class=\"italic\">This diagram <strong>is able to</strong> help you organize your ideas.</span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">This diagram <strong>was able to</strong> help you organize your ideas.</span><br/><br/> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>Simple Tenses</b><br/><br/> Simple tenses can be in either present, future or past, and commonly use the following structure: <br/><br/> <center><strong>Subject + (aux) + verb + complement.</strong></center><br/><br/> Use simple tenses to express general ideas and facts.<br/><br/> In present form, you use the auxiliary <strong>do</strong> to make questions and express negative statements. <br/>E.g.<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">You <b>need</b> to install that app.<br/> You <b>don't need</b> to install that app.<br/> <b>Do</b> you <b>need</b> to install that app?</span><br/><br/> In past form, you use the auxiliary <strong>did</strong> to make negative statements and questions.<br/>E.g.<br/> <span class=\"italic\">You <b>needed</b> to install that app.<br/> You <b>didn't need</b> to install that app.<br/> <b>Did</b> you <b>need</b> to install that app?</span><br/><br/> In future form, you use the modal auxiliary verb <strong>will</strong> to make affirmative and negative statements and questions.<br/>E.g.<br/> <span class=\"italic\">You <b>will need</b> to install this app.<br/> You <b>won't need</b> to install this app.<br/> <b>Will</b> you <b>need</b> to install this app?</span><br/><br/> "}, {"type": "text", "content": " <b>Continuous Tenses</b><br/><br/> You can use tenses in continuous form to express ongoing actions in either present, past or future times.<br/><br/> Continuous forms are characterized by the use of the verb <strong>to be</strong> as auxiliary verb and the prefix <strong>-ing</strong> after the main verb.<br/> Structure:<br/><br/> <center><strong>Subject + to be + verb + ing + complement.</strong></center><br/><br/> Present tense:<br/><br/> E.g.<br> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>I'm</strong> currently running a test on this program, to see if it works. <br><strong>Are</strong> you <strong>running</strong> a test on this terminal? <br><strong>I'm not running</strong> a test on that computer right now, you can use it.</span><br/><br/> Past tense:<br/><br/>E.g. <br><span class=\"italic\">I <strong>was sketching</strong> the designing of our next project.<br/> I <strong>wasn't thinking</strong> on that matter when I made the design.<br/> <strong>Weren't</strong> you <strong>working</strong> on that project with Alisson?</span><br/><br/> Future tense:<br/><br/> Future time is commonly used with the auxiliary <b>will + ing</b> forms.<br/><br/>E.g.<br>  <span class=\"italic\">I <strong>will be working</strong> on that design the whole day tomorrow.<br/> I <strong>won't be coming</strong> to the office tomorrow because I'll be designing the web page.<br/> <strong>Will</strong> you <strong>be working</strong> on the project tomorrow?</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "As a software analyst, one of the many functions you can have among your duties is to diagram and sketch how a program has to work. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "During the planning stage, it is important to define what modeling approach you are using to sketch the workflow of the project. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Therefore, these tools aren't able to perform the same actions as XP seeks to segment tasks in order to ease processes while improving quality. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "sketch "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make a provisional drawing about something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: One of the many functions you can have among your duties is to diagram and sketch how a program has to work."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob12aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "opt "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make a decision over something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can opt to choose the Unified Modeling Language. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob12aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "correlate "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to link 2 things; to make a relation between two objects"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It diagrams the way the workflow correlates one dependency to another. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob12aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "stakeholder "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an individual or a group of people involved with a project"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You could always opt to choose the extreme programming approach (XP), which offers a more relaxed schedule for programmers, stakeholders and individuals to accomplish their goals."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob12aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "AM Agile Modeling"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an approach to software modeling"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(acronym)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Both can relate to AM principles."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob12aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about UML and XP."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a comparative chart in which you include the most relevant similarities and differences between both modeling approaches. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a poster with your chart. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Show it to your classmates and instructor."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about UML and XP."}, {"type": "text", "content": "A create a comparative chart with the similarities and differences between both modeling approaches."}, {"type": "text", "content": "A make a poster with a comparative chart."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Sentence Variety"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p><strong>Sentence variety</strong> allows your texts to have rhythm and flow, which makes easier for readers to understand your ideas.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><br /> There are multiple techniques to create sentence variety in your texts, these are some of them:</p> <ol> <li>You can start some of your sentences by an <i>adverb</i>.</li> </ol> <p>Example:</p> <p><strong><i>Frequently</i></strong><i>, software developers use UML since it includes meta object facility and graphic notation.</i></p> <ol start=\"2\"> <li>You can join your ideas using a <i>verb ending in ING</i>.</li> </ol> <p>Example:</p> <p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">Two sentences</span></p> <p><i>UML uses object-oriented programming. It allows programmers to create visual models of systems.</i></p> <p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">Joined with ING form</span></p> <p><i>UML allows programmers to create visual models of systems <strong>using</strong> object-oriented programming.</i></p> <ol start=\"3\"> <li>You can join your ideas using a <i>verb in past participle</i>.</li> </ol> <p>Example:</p> <p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">Two sentences</span></p> <p><i>UML executables could run entire applications. &nbsp;They were deployed in different types of platforms.&nbsp; </i></p> <p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">Joined with a past participle</span></p> <p><strong><i>Deployed</i></strong><i> in different types of applications, UML executables could run entire applications. </i></p>"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can read about XP and UML."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can write a text to summarize ideas related to XP and UML."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can give details about XP and UML in a written text."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What steps should we take into account when designing software?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you think it is possible to mix and use different developing models interchangeably? Why?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "Choosing the Right Developing Model"}], "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "img_path": "", "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "_data": [{"