t": 1, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 0}], "id": 0}], "type": "DWS", "template_override": 1, "id": 12}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob12re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "As a software analyst, one of the many functions you can have among your duties is to diagram and sketch how a program has to work. To do so, it is important to know what the program or application has to do, and what individuals or areas of the company are responsible for it to work. <br/><br/>"}, {"time": "21500", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob12re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "During the planning stage, it is important to define what modeling approach you are using to sketch the workflow of the project. Among the most common ones to work with, you can opt to choose the Unified Modeling Language (UML), which allows you to diagram and model the way the software creation process should be carried out, as it diagrams the way the workflow correlates one dependency to another. Or you could always opt to choose the eXtreme Programming approach (XP), which offers a more relaxed schedule for programmers, stakeholders and individuals to accomplish their goals, as the commonly end-of-day practice can be changed or modified to an end-of-week assignment.<br/><br/>"}, {"time": "71000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob12re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Same way, some people can agree with the fact that both approaches are essential when designing software products as they both can relate to AM (Agile Modeling) principles; however, some others consider it is advisable not to combine them because they are both alike in some aspects. Such claims are based on the fact that UML and XP have similar tools, such as use cases and user stories; therefore, these tools aren't able to perform the same actions as XP seeks to segment tasks in order to ease processes while improving quality. <br/><br/>\nAs an overall, you must have clear what you are aiming your software to do in order to use either XP or UML as a modeling approach."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n4le02ob12re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le02ob12re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "static", "template_override": 1, "id": 2, "contents": [{"value": "01.jpg", "type": "img"}, {"value": "02.jpg", "type": "img"}, {"value": "03.jpg", "type": "img"}]}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "video", "template_override": 1, "id": 3, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n4le02ob12re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}